  "text": "Now put a whole month together: four workloads, each with its own model and volume. Code review on Opus looks like the big line. [curious] It isn't. Document summaries on Sonnet are, at eight hundred sixty-four dollars a month, because volume and tokens multiply. The whole month comes to about seventeen hundred and sixty dollars. So once you can see the bill, how do you shrink it?\n\nSix levers, in the order they usually pay off. First, route each task to the cheapest model that passes. Fifty thousand classifications a day cost four thousand and fifty dollars a month on Opus, and a hundred and one on Haiku. Second, cache the stable prefix: whatever never changes goes first. That forty-turn chat drops from a dollar nineteen to twenty-six cents. Third, cut output, not input. Each output token you save is worth five input ones. Fourth, turn reasoning effort down to the lowest level that passes. Fifth, batch anything nobody's waiting on, at about half off. And only then, trim the prompt. Notice the word that came up twice: passes. Passes what? That takes a test, and that's next: measuring whether a model is good enough.",
